# SYNOPSIS

```text
page NAME SECTION
summary ...
[names ...] [src ...] [keys ...] [lang swift|c|rc] [host]

# HEADING

Paragraph text, with `literal`, <param>, name(N) and {label|target}.

@node ID TITLE
```

# DESCRIPTION

A page of the manual is one UTF-8 file in guide, the format this page describes.
It holds a header, then a body of lines, each line's kind decided by how it begins, so a reader needs no more than the current line and whether it is inside a fence.
The page reads well with `cat`; vx-man(1) refills it to the terminal's width.
Every reader of the format parses it with `system/guide`, and nothing else does.

The parser is strict.
What it doesn't know it refuses, with the file and the line, and it never guesses: a typo in a directive, a fence's kind or a link's target is an error, not a page shown wrongly.
A page that isn't UTF-8 is refused too.

A page may start with the line `@guide 1`.
It names the version of the format; a page without it is version 1, permanently.
A later version is a new number, and a parser refuses one it doesn't know.

@node header The header

The header is directives, as a manifest(6) is: one a line, a name and then words, `#` starting a comment.
It ends at the first blank line.

: `page`
  The page's name and its section, `1` to `8`.
  The name is lower case: a letter or digit, then letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `+` or `-`.
  It is also the file's name, `man/<section>/<name>`.
  It comes first.
: `summary`
  The rest of the line: one line for NAME and the index, with no full stop.
: `names`
  Every name the page documents, besides its own when it gives none: each is an index entry and a link target.
  It may repeat.
: `src`
  The paths in the tree that SOURCE lists.
: `keys`
  More words to find the page by.
: `lang`
  `swift`, `c` or `rc`: the language of a section 2 page's interface.
: `host`
  The page's program runs on the dev machine, not on VectraOS.

`page` and `summary` are required, and `page`, `summary`, `lang` and `host` come once.
The author never writes NAME or SOURCE: they are made from the header, and a heading of either is refused.

@node blocks Blocks

A body line is one of these, by how it starts:

| Start | Is |
| (blank) | the end of a paragraph, list item or definition |
| `# ` | a section heading |
| `## ` | a subsection heading |
| `- ` | a list item |
| `: ` | a definition's term, described by the indented lines after it |
| two spaces | a continuation of the list item or definition above |
| `|` | a table row; the first row of a run is the header |
| ```` ``` ```` | a fence |
| `@` | a directive |
| anything else | paragraph text, its lines joined and refilled |

Headings are in capitals, and Plan 9's keep Plan 9's order: SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, EXAMPLES, FILES, SEE ALSO, DIAGNOSTICS, BUGS.
A heading of the author's own is in capitals too.
A line starting `#` that isn't `# ` or `## ` is an error.

There is no nesting.
A list item holds no list and a definition no fence; a page that wants more structure wants a node.
A continuation line with nothing above to continue is an error.

A table row's cells are separated by `|`; a `|` inside a literal belongs to its cell, and the row's last `|` may be left off.
A run of rows is one table, its columns as wide as their widest cells.

@node fences Fences

A line of three backticks, followed by a kind or by nothing, opens a fence; a line of three backticks alone closes it.
What lies between is shown exactly as written, never refilled, and must fit 80 columns.
A fence that is never closed is an error.
The kinds are:

: `usage`
  A command's synopsis in Plan 9's notation: the command and its flags literal, every other word a parameter, `[ ]` optional and `...` repeated.
: `swift`
  Swift: declarations in a section 2 SYNOPSIS, or example code.
: `c`
  C: declarations in a section 2 SYNOPSIS, or example code.
: `rc`
  A transcript or a script; lines starting with `%` and a space are typed, the others are output.
: `text`
  Anything: a diagram, a table of bytes.
  A fence with no kind is the same.

Any other kind is an error.

@node inline Text inside a line

Four things are special in headings, paragraphs, list items, definitions and table cells, and nothing else is:

: `` `cat -u` ``
  A literal: what is typed, a file's name, code.
  A literal opened by <n> backticks closes at the next run of exactly <n>, so one containing a backtick is opened by more; with two or more, one space just inside each end is dropped, so a literal may start with a backtick.
  An unclosed literal is an error.
: `<file>`
  A parameter: `<`, a letter, then letters, digits, `.`, `_` or `-`, then `>`.
: `rc(1)`
  A link to a page: a name followed by a section number in parentheses.
: `{label|target}`, `{target}`
  A link with words of its own, or to a node, or out of the manual.
  It is shown as `label (target)`, or as the target alone.

A link's target is `name(N)`, `name(N)#node`, `#node` for a node of the same page, or a URL whose scheme is `https:`, `gemini:` or `gopher:`.
Any other target, and a `{` with no `}`, is an error.
A literal `{`, a `<word>` that is not a parameter, or a `name(2)` that is not a link goes inside backticks.
There is no emphasis.

@node nodes Nodes

A page is one node unless it says otherwise.
A line `@node <id> <title>` starts a node, its id lower-case letters, digits and `-`, unique in the page, and its title, the rest of the line, what a contents list shows.
Text before the first node is the main node, which every page has, and which holds SYNOPSIS and DESCRIPTION.

Reading order is the file's order, so a page split into nodes still prints, greps and pipes as one text: `vx man guide` prints every node, and `vx man guide fences` prints one.
Nodes are for long pages; a short page has none.

`@node` is the only directive in the body, and `@guide` is allowed only on the first line.

# EXAMPLES

A page, whole:

```text
page cat 1
summary concatenate files
src shell/commands/lib/Commands.swift

# SYNOPSIS

```

followed by a `usage` fence holding `cat [file ...]`, then:

```text
# DESCRIPTION

`cat` reads each <file> in order and writes it to standard
output. With no <file> it reads standard input.

# SEE ALSO

ls(1)
```

# FILES

: `man/<section>/<page>`
  The pages, in the tree.

# SEE ALSO

vx-man(1), manifest(6)

# DIAGNOSTICS

The parser reports the first error it meets with its file and line, such as `man/1/cat:4: a fence kind guide doesn't know`, and stops.

# BUGS

A fence cannot show a line of three backticks alone, since that line closes it; the example above is split in two for that reason.
